  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will translate eastward across the Central \nPlains Sunday. This should bring a mainly dry day, with near \nseasonal average high temperatures in the mid to upper 70s. \nSouthwest flow aloft begins to strengthen Monday ahead of a closed \nupper trough that will digging southeastward toward the Desert \nSouthwest. Leeward troughing will deepen during the day with a moist \nsoutheast low-levek flow in place to the east of the troughing \nacross the entire area. Surface dew points around 60F should be in \nplace, which is rather impressive for the end of September. PWATS \nwill be on the increase once again due to a combination of low-level \nmoisture from the Gulf and mid and upper-level sub-tropical moisture \nbeing pulled northeastward from northwest Mexico and the Desert \nSouthwest. ECMWF ENS average mean PWATS will be in the 1.1 to 1.3 \nrange Monday afternoon. Convection is likely to develop near the \nleeward trough within the moist up-slope flow regime. With continued \nmoisture advection on a modest southerly low-level jet, it appears \nthat the convection will grow upscale as it moves eastward across \nthe area Monday night. Locally heavy rainfall rates could become a \nconcern due to the anomalously high amount of moisture that will be \nin place. \n  \nAs we move toward the middle of the week the weather remains \ninteresting. The Desert Southwest closed upper trough will swing \neastward toward the Southern Plains. At the same time, the remnants \nof Hurricane Polo and it's associated tropical moisture, will be \npulled northeast and become absorbed into the upper circulation of \nthe upper low. There is low confidence on how this could affect us. \nThe consensus seems to be that most of the rainfall (potentially \nheavy) will focus south and southeast of our area Wednesday and \nThursday. This is largely due to strong northern stream energy \nacross the Northern Plains, which keeps the upper low tracking in a \nmore easterly direction across the Southern Plains. Of note, the GFS \nis weaker with the northern stream, and allows for rainfall to wrap \nback westward into at least the eastern half of the area. Will \ncontinue to monitor.",
